    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d370">

                <pb facs="0566" n="280" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg280"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.0012/680</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-07-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 24, 1916, noon</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received July 25, 1.10 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4149. Think there is remote possibility that Germany might now sign Bryan
                    arbitration treaty. Shall I pursue matter? If so, advise matter be kept secret
                    so that if successful we may announce it suddenly. Bernstorff should not be informed as, if
                    treaty is signed, <pb facs="0567" n="281" xml:id="pg281"/> think Chancellor
                    wants to do everything here. Of course suggest possibility Germany signing
                    treaty and then starting submarine war. Please inform Colonel House.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
